{
  "data": {
    "text": "To explore the brain networks at the macrolevel, the AAL atlas was utilized to segment each subject’s whole-brain rs-fMRI into 116 regions (90 cortical/subcortical regions [45 for each hemisphere] and 26 cerebellar/vermis regions). Within each AAL region, an average time series was calculated.\n\nFrom the time series matrix of size 1200 · 116 (time points · AAL regions), FC matrices (116 · 116) were derived for each subject by applying Pearson correlation across the whole duration of the time series. All FC were then Fisher’s z-transformed.\n\nEach 116 · 116 FC matrix was thresholded and converted to a binary adjacency matrix for graph theoretic analyses. Therefore, to ensure the most direct mathematical comparability of graph properties across subjects, a proportion threshold based on graph density was applied to each FC matrix, where density threshold ranged from 0.05 to 0.95 at 0.05 intervals. For example, when a density threshold of 0.1 is applied, for each subject, the top 10% of the FC are retained and the FC matrix is converted to a binary adjacency matrix. Different thresholds were applied to compare group differences of graph properties at various graph densities.\n\nNetwork measures in this study were derived using the Brain Connectivity Toolbox (Rubinov and Sporns, 2010). Nodal clustering coefficient and nodal local efficiency were adopted to examine the regional characteristics of the functional brain network. For the global network matrices, the network clustering coefficient (C) and the characteristic path length (L) were calculated. The small-worldness metric of a network (r) was then estimated as the ratio between the normalized clustering coefficient and the normalized characteristic path length: r = C/Crand / (L/Lrand), where Crand and Lrand are the average of C and L derived from 30 corresponding random networks generated by rewiring each edge approximately 10 times while preserving the original degree distribution."
  },
